+
+## Exact behavioral changes
+
+1. `src/virtuoso_bridge/transport/tunnel.py`, `SSHClient.is_running`
+   - A reachable forwarded port remains the primary liveness evidence.
+   - Windows no longer calls the POSIX-style `os.kill(pid, 0)` fallback after
+     the saved port is unreachable. The state is treated as stale so startup
+     can replace it.
+   - POSIX PID probing defensively treats `SystemError` as a failed probe.
+2. `src/virtuoso_bridge/transport/ssh.py`, `SSHRunner.stop_port_forward` and
+   `SSHRunner.is_tunnel_alive`
+   - Stale/invalid Windows PID operations now also catch `SystemError`, matching
+     the observed Python-on-Windows failure shape.
+3. `src/virtuoso_bridge/virtuoso/basic/bridge.py`, `VirtuosoClient.from_env`
+   - The documented no-running-tunnel branch now actually calls `SSHClient.warm()`
+     before returning a client bound to its selected local port.
+4. `src/virtuoso_bridge/transport/ssh.py`, idempotent transport retries
+   - Existing SSH command/upload/download retries now wait 1 second and 3 seconds
+     between transient failures instead of retrying three times immediately.
+   - Generic connect timeout is transient; authentication, host-key, DNS, and
+     other deterministic configuration errors still fail without retry.
+5. `src/virtuoso_bridge/virtuoso/basic/bridge.py`, pre-send recovery
+   - A private exception marks only `connect()` refusal before `sendall()`.
+   - A managed client may warm its tunnel and retry exactly once at that point.
+     Errors after any SKILL payload could have been sent are never replayed.
+6. Tests
+   - `tests/test_tunnel_recovery.py` covers unreachable Windows state, defensive
+     POSIX probe handling, and runner PID cleanup.
+   - `tests/test_profile_resolver.py` verifies that `from_env` warms a newly
+     created tunnel and uses the warmed port.
+   - `tests/test_ssh_retry.py` covers paced transient retries, immediate failure
+     for non-transient errors, and the existing ControlMaster fallback.
+   - `tests/test_virtuoso_tunnel_recovery.py` proves pre-send-only tagging,
+     one recovery attempt, no replay for unmarked errors, and warm failure.
+7. `src/virtuoso_bridge/transport/ssh.py`, Windows tunnel creation flags
+   - The persistent tunnel keeps `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`, `SW_HIDE`, and
+     `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP`.
+   - It no longer adds `DETACHED_PROCESS`; Windows ignores
+     `CREATE_NO_WINDOW` when those two flags are combined, which caused the
+     native OpenSSH tunnel to open a Windows Terminal tab.
+   - Normal Windows child lifetime already lets the tunnel survive the
+     short-lived Bridge/VDA launcher. No SSH command, jump-host rule, or
+     tunnel state behavior changed.
+8. `tests/test_windows_process_flags.py`
+   - Portably asserts the exact hidden tunnel flags, including the absence of
+     `DETACHED_PROCESS`.
+
+No environment variable, state-file schema, JSON protocol, remote directory,
+SSH authentication rule, SKILL payload, Spectre implementation, or VDA worker
+protocol was changed. No keepalive setting was added. A reset after payload
+send remains checkpoint territory and is not claimed as safe to replay.
+
+This paragraph describes the transport patches through `cd9aa97`. The later
+`b1194ca` request-framing addendum below deliberately changes only how the same
+single JSON request boundary is detected on Windows SSH forwards.
